{#
  FRICTION CHART — Figma 349:17196, built as SVG rather than shipped as a
  picture. It is two series over five stages: a line chart. As SVG it stays
  sharp at any width, takes its colours from the page's own tokens, and can
  carry a description a screen reader can actually use — none of which a PNG of
  a chart can do.

  THE VIEWBOX IS THE COMP'S PLOT BOX. 1206 x 384 with six gridlines 72 apart, so
  the geometry below is the comp's and not a re-invention of it. Each gridline
  row in the comp is a 24-tall instance holding a 10px-wide y label at x=0 and a
  rule that starts at x=22 — which is why the rules here start at 22 and the
  labels sit centred on x=5, rather than the plot running edge to edge.

  Y MAPS 5 AT THE TOP TO 0 AT THE BOTTOM — "higher = more friction", as the
  comp's own note says. That is the opposite of the usual "up is good", which is
  why the note is on the chart rather than left implicit.

  BOTH SERIES ARE DASHED in the comp, and that is doing work: the two lines
  cross at Join, and dashes let the one underneath stay readable through the one
  on top. The previous flow is an alpha grey for the same reason — the gridlines
  read through it.

  ACCESSIBILITY: the <svg> carries role="img" and a <title>/<desc> pair, and the
  numbers are ALSO in a visually-hidden table below it. A line chart is a picture
  of a table; anyone who cannot see the picture should still get the table rather
  than a one-line summary of it.
#}

{%- set c = challenquiz.why.chart -%}
{%- set W = 1206 -%}
{%- set H = 384 -%}
{%- set STEP = 72 -%}
{%- set AXIS = 22 -%}

{# x centres: the comp spaces five stages across the plot at 241.2 apart,
   starting half a step in, which puts them at 121, 362, 603, 844, 1085. #}
{%- macro px(i) %}{{ (121 + (i * 241.2)) | round(1) }}{% endmacro %}
{%- macro py(v) %}{{ (12 + ((5 - v) * STEP)) | round(1) }}{% endmacro %}

<figure class="case-chart">
  <figcaption class="case-chart__head">
    <ul class="case-chart__legend">
      {%- for l in c.legend %}
        <li class="case-chart__legend-item">
          <span class="case-chart__swatch case-chart__swatch--{{ l.key }}" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          {{ l.label }}
        </li>
      {%- endfor %}
    </ul>
    <p class="case-chart__note">{{ c.note }}</p>
  </figcaption>

  <svg
    class="case-chart__svg"
    viewBox="0 0 {{ W }} {{ H }}"
    role="img"
    aria-labelledby="cq-chart-title cq-chart-desc"
  >
    <title id="cq-chart-title">Friction across the five stages of a game, before and after the redesign</title>
    <desc id="cq-chart-desc">
      A 0 to 5 friction index over five stages. The previous flow peaks at Start
      and Result; the redesign is lower at every stage, and closest to the old
      flow at Join, where the wallet gate remains.
    </desc>

    {#- Gridlines and their y labels. The rule starts clear of the label. -#}
    <g class="case-chart__grid">
      {%- for v in [5, 4, 3, 2, 1, 0] %}
        <line x1="{{ AXIS }}" x2="{{ W }}" y1="{{ py(v) }}" y2="{{ py(v) }}" />
      {%- endfor %}
    </g>
    <g class="case-chart__ylabels">
      {%- for v in [5, 4, 3, 2, 1, 0] %}
        {#- +6 puts the baseline on the rule rather than the cap-height. -#}
        <text x="5" y="{{ py(v) | float + 6 }}" text-anchor="middle">{{ v }}</text>
      {%- endfor %}
    </g>

    {#- One dashed polyline plus its points per series. Previous first, so the
        redesign sits on top where they cross at Join. -#}
    {%- for key in ["previous", "redesign"] %}
      {%- set data = c.series[key] %}
      <polyline
        class="case-chart__line case-chart__line--{{ key }}"
        points="{%- for v in data %}{{ px(loop.index0) }},{{ py(v) }} {% endfor -%}"
      />
      {%- for v in data %}
        <circle
          class="case-chart__dot case-chart__dot--{{ key }}"
          cx="{{ px(loop.index0) }}" cy="{{ py(v) }}" r="6"
        />
      {%- endfor %}
    {%- endfor %}

    {#- The annotation: a dashed ring around the point the redesign could not
        fix, and its label centred under it — 26px across in the comp, so r=13
        against the dot's r=6. -#}
    {%- set av = c.series[c.annotation.series][c.annotation.stage] %}
    <circle
      class="case-chart__ring"
      cx="{{ px(c.annotation.stage) }}" cy="{{ py(av) }}" r="13"
    />
    <text
      class="case-chart__annotation"
      x="{{ px(c.annotation.stage) }}" y="{{ py(av) | float + 37 }}"
      text-anchor="middle"
    >{{ c.annotation.label }}</text>
  </svg>

  {#- The x axis sits outside the SVG so its type is real text at the page's own
      size rather than SVG text that scales with the viewBox. Numbered, as the
      comp has it — an ordered list, because the five stages are a sequence. -#}
  <ol class="case-chart__axis">
    {%- for s in c.stages %}
      <li>{{ loop.index }}. {{ s }}</li>
    {%- endfor %}
  </ol>

  {#- The same numbers, for anyone not reading the picture. -#}
  <table class="case-chart__data">
    <caption>Friction index by stage, 0–5</caption>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th scope="col">Stage</th>
        {%- for l in c.legend %}<th scope="col">{{ l.label }}</th>{% endfor %}
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {%- for s in c.stages %}
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">{{ s }}</th>
          {%- for l in c.legend %}<td>{{ c.series[l.key][loop.parent.loop.index0] }}</td>{% endfor %}
        </tr>
      {%- endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p class="case-chart__caption">{{ c.caption }}</p>
</figure>
